the blue instance's action is a separate node with its own belief q about it. With a symmetric prior of 1/2, both expected values tie, and the same canonical tie-break makes both instances pick X, which yields nothing. Asymmetric beliefs don't settle it either, since the only thing distinguishing the two instances is the input label.\n\nNext I'm checking the red/blue case with a fixed belief about the blue instance's action, and noticing that with symmetric beliefs the tie-break can't separate the two instances. The payoff depends only on the input label, which action-level FDT reaches only through its beliefs, so I'm noting that self-referential fixed-point issue, which policy selection avoids by construction. I'm also flagging that the source's fixed belief q \"doesn't matter\" in transparent Newcomb but would matter in coordination.\n\nI'm noting that Newcomb-style results also depend on how the same reasoning is applied across the whole agent, so the footnote's phrasing about where FDT changes its intervention point matters for the essay's framing.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":83949,"completion_tokens":12774,"total_tokens":96723,"cost":0.591276,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.591276,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.335796,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.25548},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4997,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}